; The regulated workflow's two properties, re-asked as derivations (extension
; §2) for `modality-cross-check.sh`. Both are `live`, so BOTH relations below
; are COUNTEREXAMPLE SETS and both are expected to be EMPTY.
;
; Why counterexamples. `live F` is AG EF F — a statement about every reachable
; situation. Stratified Datalog cannot assert a universal positively without a
; nullary relation, which extension §3 rejects outright, so the question is
; asked in its refutable form instead: derive the situations that WITNESS a
; failure. Empty answer set ⟺ the property holds. The refutable form is also
; the useful one — a failure hands `writ derive --why` a concrete stranded
; situation to explain, where a yes/no flag would hand it nothing.
;
; A green cross-check on this file therefore means "no case is ever stranded"
; and "the assignee slot can always be refilled". These two, with
; oversight/accountability, are also the repo's inversion detectors: they are
; the `live` properties that HOLD, so their counterexample set is empty while
; their satisfying set is not, and re-pointing `settle-able` at
; `can-still-settle` flips the derived verdict to `fails` against a `writ check`
; that says `holds`. (Where a `live` property fails — river/no-blunders,
; access/revocation-possible — both sets are non-empty and an inversion there
; is invisible to the oracle. Hence these comments.)

(load "ct.rules")

; Can a case get stuck? The escalation gap is a non-F terminal for both
; implementations: a gap edge has no successor, so neither `edge` nor
; `Space.bwd_reach` traverses it.
(satisfies settled (is claim-1.stage settled))
(can-reach can-still-settle settled)
(trapped settle-able can-still-settle)

; May the vacatable assignee slot always be refilled? The same three lines
; against a different guard, which is the whole reason they are forms.
(satisfies assigned (defined claim-1.assignee))
(can-reach can-still-assign assigned)
(trapped assignee-fillable can-still-assign)
